    "summary": "High‑energy '89 Mecca show with killer Half Step and Blow Away",
    "blurb": "The set bursts open with a driving Mississippi Half‑Step Uptown Toodeloo and an inspired Blow Away featuring Brent’s memorable rap. Highlights include a tight Half Step jam, powerful renditions of Franklin’s Tower and Morning Dew, and a fiery second‑set flow from China Cat Sunflower into All Along the Watchtower.",
    "review": "On April 15, 1989 at The Mecca, the Dead delivered a concise yet electrifying performance. Jerry Garcia matched Phil Lesh’s fire, laying down solid lead work and an especially strong opening solo on Mississippi Half‑Step Uptown Toodeloo, though his vocal on Terrapin Station was noted as weaker than usual. Phil’s bass was “on fire” throughout, anchoring the band’s high‑energy momentum. Brent Mydland stole several moments: his rap on Blow Away split fans but remains unforgettable, and his keyboards drove the exuberant Half Step jam and lit up the second set. Bob Weir’s rhythm guitar and vocals stayed audible and well‑placed, adding to the tight ensemble feel. The show’s flow kept the foot on the accelerator from start to finish, with standout moments like Franklin’s Tower, Morning Dew, and a blazing All Along the Watchtower that capped an already potent set.\n\nRecording-wise, the best listening experience comes from the GEMS soundboard capture (gd1989-04-15.sbd.healy.gems.80277.flac16), praised for its crystal‑clear dynamics and ability to convey the band’s energy without noticeable flaws. While a matrix source offers a complete show, it suffers from tape hiss and a fast‑pitch anomaly that detracts from the overall sound quality.",
